from reading_extensions import EXTENSIONS

ADDITIONS={
41:("At the next staff meeting, the manager used an invented example to check that everyone understood the new approval field. Staff had to distinguish permission to inspect from permission to replace a part. The exercise revealed one remaining ambiguity about unanswered calls. The rule was clarified: leaving a voicemail requesting consent was not the same as receiving consent, even when the proposed work appeared sensible.", "The writer also checked the date by which the revised payment would appear. A promised adjustment and a completed refund were separate events. She retained the correspondence until the account reflected the agreement, then closed the complaint. This final check was a practical part of resolution, not an attempt to prolong a disagreement after the shop had made a reasonable response."),
42:("The provider offered a free introductory presentation in the meantime, clearly identifying it as information rather than a substitute for the practical course. The applicant attended to understand the teaching style. She still kept the later equipment and timetable questions open. A positive impression of a teacher can inform a decision, but it cannot remove a schedule conflict or supply a camera on a date when none is available.", "The follow-up also asked whether the quoted printing amount covered a fixed number of images or varied with each project. This distinction affected the likely total. The provider supplied a typical range and identified it as an estimate. The learner could then budget with an allowance for variation instead of interpreting a sample figure as a guaranteed maximum cost for every possible assignment."),
43:("On Tuesday, reception knew which supplier to expect and could direct the driver to the usable lift. The customer had also checked that protective covers would fit through the corridor. These preparations did not require a long letter, but they depended on the essential information being accurate. The written request initiated coordination; the successful delivery showed that the revised arrangement had actually been understood and carried out.", "She read the message once from the scheduler\'s perspective, asking whether the recipient could update the booking without guessing. This revealed that the floor number was missing. Adding it was more useful than replacing a simple word with a formal synonym. The final review therefore focused on the decisions the reader needed to make and the information required to make them correctly."),
44:("Management also published the booth\'s booking history as anonymous occupancy totals. It did not reveal which clients tenants had called or what they discussed. The review needed evidence of demand and usability, not the contents of private conversations. Choosing proportionate information protected the purpose of the facility while still allowing the owners to evaluate whether further investment in another booth would be justified.", "The writer offered to help collect anonymous reports of failed booking attempts, with a clear end date for the exercise. She did not offer to monitor other tenants\' calls. This made the contribution specific and proportionate. It also showed that a tenant could support the investigation without taking over management responsibilities or collecting information unrelated to deciding how much additional private space was needed."),
45:("A later report should also examine whether benefits continue when initial publicity fades. People may try a discounted service once without adopting it for regular journeys. Repeat use, access to important destinations and persistent crowding provide different information from an opening-week passenger total. The distinction helps authorities decide whether the offer created a sustainable improvement or mainly a temporary increase in curiosity about the service.", "The writer can also identify what information would change this judgement. For example, evidence that a reliable route remains unused mainly because of cost would strengthen the case for discounts. Evidence of substantial unmet demand after the final departure would favor timetable investment. Naming such conditions shows that the recommendation follows from reasons and can respond to evidence rather than expressing a fixed preference regardless of local circumstances."),
46:("The same principle applies to a course calendar. Reaching the last week should open a review of evidence rather than trigger a message declaring mastery automatically. Users can be congratulated for sustained effort while still seeing unresolved needs. Encouragement and accuracy are compatible when progress messages describe the achievement actually observed, such as completing a revision cycle, instead of assigning an external level that has not been measured.", "Once a month, I compare an early and a recent recording on different but similarly demanding topics. I listen for clearer organization and better responses to follow-up questions, not just fewer pauses in a familiar speech. The comparison is still informal, so I record its limitations. It nevertheless gives me a more useful direction for practice than a streak counter showing that I opened the app on consecutive days."),
47:("Reviewing the arrangement afterwards should include the employee\'s experience as well as the employer\'s chosen metric. A course may improve a report but impose an unexpectedly heavy workload outside working hours. That information can guide future scheduling or provider selection. Training policy is stronger when it learns from implementation instead of treating payment of a fee and receipt of a certificate as the end of every responsibility.", "The employee would also preserve the approved scope if a later course offered additional modules. An initial agreement to fund one course did not authorize every optional extension. Asking about the extra cost before enrolling would maintain the same clarity requested from the employer. Mutual responsibility means both sides distinguish an approved commitment from a potentially useful addition that still requires discussion."),
48:("Residents should know how to report problems and which organization is responsible for responding. A route may involve the developer, railway operator and highway authority, creating opportunities for complaints to be passed between them. A published coordination arrangement helps prevent this. Shared responsibility need not mean no one is accountable; it should identify who leads a response and how the other organizations contribute when a problem crosses boundaries.", "If the council proposed a different crossing location, the writer would assess whether it met the original access need rather than reject it simply because it differed from her suggestion. She would check the route length, waiting conditions and connection to the station entrance. Returning to the underlying criteria makes consultation a way to improve a decision, rather than a competition over whose drawing appears in the final plan."),
49:("Residents and businesses also need a predictable opportunity to review the policy after implementation. A consultation held only before a change cannot capture every consequence that appears later. An ongoing review should remain focused and proportionate, with clear questions and a published response. This allows the destination to adjust while avoiding a permanent series of meetings with no visible connection between the comments received and the actions eventually taken.", "We made the review available in a short public summary, with a separate technical appendix for readers wanting more detail. This helped visitors understand the practical changes without requiring them to interpret every table. Clear presentation was part of accountability: publishing information is less useful if the intended audience cannot find the conclusion, the remaining limitations or the next action the museum has committed to take."),
50:("A public summary would explain which changes were supported, which remained uncertain and what would happen next. It would distinguish a decision to continue testing from permanent approval. This mattered to traders planning deliveries and to residents arranging travel. Accurate wording about the status of a trial is part of implementation, because people make real plans based on whether an arrangement is temporary, confirmed or merely being considered.", "The writer also stated that a result unfavorable to her preferred option should be reported openly. If the collection arrangement created new access problems, it would need revision. This made her support conditional on the public purpose of the trial rather than on obtaining a particular outcome. A persuasive consultation response can advocate a position while remaining willing to reconsider it when relevant evidence warrants a change.")
}
for week, additions in ADDITIONS.items():
    EXTENSIONS[week]=tuple(text+' '+addition for text,addition in zip(EXTENSIONS[week],additions))
